WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:05.173
Follow one nitrogen atom.

2
00:00:06.453 --> 00:00:09.313
Once it is part of a soybean leaf, is it there for good?

3
00:00:10.553 --> 00:00:11.673
Or does it keep on moving?

4
00:00:12.817 --> 00:00:17.737
In the first film, nitrogen gas stayed locked until a changer opened it.

5
00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:21.117
Now follow one atom that a nodule changed.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.420
Here is our atom, one half of a grey pair in the air.

7
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.320
A nodule changes it into usable nitrogen.

8
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.840
The plant builds it into a protein in a leaf.

9
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.860
A white-tailed deer wanders over and eats the leaf.

10
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.820
The deer digests the leaf's proteins.

11
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.140
Our atom becomes part of a protein in the deer's body.

12
00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.800
Animals get nitrogen by eating plants or other animals.

13
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.943
Later, the deer dies in the woods.

14
00:00:53.243 --> 00:00:55.383
Is its nitrogen gone for good, or not?

15
00:00:56.663 --> 00:00:57.663
Pause and decide.

16
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:05.707
No, it is not gone.

17
00:01:07.207 --> 00:01:08.387
Decomposers go to work.

18
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:14.887
A decomposer is a living thing, such as a fungus or a bacterium, that breaks down

19
00:01:14.887 --> 00:01:16.147
dead things and wastes.

20
00:01:17.217 --> 00:01:20.837
Decomposers break down the deer's body and its droppings.

21
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:27.377
Our atom returns to the soil as usable nitrogen, one green dot in the soil water.

22
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:30.787
A corn root reaches it and takes it up.

23
00:01:31.947 --> 00:01:33.387
Same atom, second plant.

24
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:38.727
The corn builds our atom into a brand new protein in one of its leaves.

25
00:01:39.817 --> 00:01:41.597
Later, the corn plant dies.

26
00:01:43.117 --> 00:01:47.057
Decomposers return our atom to the soil as one usable form.

27
00:01:48.357 --> 00:01:53.217
In soil with oxygen, other bacteria change it into a second usable form.

28
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:58.070
Then heavy rain soaks the soil, and little oxygen is left.

29
00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.290
Different bacteria change that second form back into nitrogen gas.

30
00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.530
Our atom rises into the air again.

31
00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:12.553
This loop, from the air, through the soil and living things, and back to the air, is

32
00:02:12.553 --> 00:02:13.553
the nitrogen cycle.

33
00:02:14.793 --> 00:02:17.033
At every step, our atom changed form.

34
00:02:18.213 --> 00:02:20.373
It was never made, and never destroyed.

35
00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:22.857
Now a second case.

36
00:02:24.157 --> 00:02:27.777
A farmer spreads far more fertilizer than the corn can take up.

37
00:02:28.857 --> 00:02:31.797
The pool of green dots fills, and then overflows.

38
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.400
Then a heavy rain falls.

39
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.260
Water moving over and through the soil carries the extra green dots into a stream.

40
00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.640
The stream flows toward the Chesapeake Bay.

41
00:02:44.683 --> 00:02:50.083
In the Bay, the extra nitrogen feeds an algal bloom, a fast growth of algae in the

42
00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:54.023
water. The water near the surface turns thick and green.

43
00:02:55.117 --> 00:02:59.857
So nitrogen goes around the loop, changing form, and is never used up.

44
00:03:00.937 --> 00:03:03.857
But water can carry it out of a field, and into the Bay.
